1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,123
Fslmaths and fslmeants often appear
together, but they do different jobs.

2
00:00:06,123 --> 00:00:11,690
Fslmaths applies operations in sequence
and writes a new image.

3
00:00:11,690 --> 00:00:17,813
Fslmeants uses a mask to summarize a
four-dimensional time series across

4
00:00:17,813 --> 00:00:18,926
selected voxels.

5
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:25,438
Fslmaths reads an input, applies
operations from left to right, and writes

6
00:00:25,438 --> 00:00:26,378
an output.

7
00:00:26,378 --> 00:00:29,667
A threshold removes values below a cutoff.

8
00:00:29,667 --> 00:00:32,956
Binarization converts the remaining
selection to ones.

9
00:00:32,956 --> 00:00:39,534
The output is a new mask, not a label
permanently attached to the original

10
00:00:39,534 --> 00:00:40,004
image.

11
00:00:40,867 --> 00:00:46,379
This example thresholds a statistical
image, binarizes the result, and saves a

12
00:00:46,379 --> 00:00:46,839
mask.

13
00:00:46,839 --> 00:00:52,811
Fslmeants then extracts the mean BOLD
value inside that mask for every volume.

14
00:00:52,811 --> 00:00:59,702
The output is a time series whose meaning
depends entirely on the mask and source

15
00:00:59,702 --> 00:01:00,161
image.

16
00:01:01,033 --> 00:01:05,226
Two files can share dimensions while
representing different anatomy.

17
00:01:05,226 --> 00:01:11,747
Check the headers, then overlay the mask
on the exact image used for extraction.

18
00:01:11,747 --> 00:01:13,611
Confirm coverage across slices.

19
00:01:13,611 --> 00:01:19,201
A time series extracted from the wrong
space is precise but meaningless.

20
00:01:20,067 --> 00:01:26,954
Use fslmaths to create a derived image and
fslmeants to measure a time series within

21
00:01:26,954 --> 00:01:27,872
a mask.

22
00:01:27,872 --> 00:01:34,300
Preserve the operation order, inspect the
mask in the source space, and only then

23
00:01:34,300 --> 00:01:36,136
interpret the extracted values.
